 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Blocks",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The chip holds three parts: a horn driver, a boost regulator and the horn-enable logic."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Horn driver",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "A push-pull output stage that drives a three-terminal piezo horn, or a two-terminal horn modified for it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Horn enable, RE46C317",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "HRNEN reads three levels. Left floating (mid-supply) it puts the chip in standby with every circuit off, the lowest-current state. Held low it runs only the boost regulator, bringing VO up to about 10 V with the horn off; this boost-only state can serve for a low-battery check. Held high it runs both the regulator and the horn driver."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Horn enable, RE46C318",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "HRNEN is a plain two-level input. Low turns off both the regulator and the horn driver (standby, lowest current); high turns both on."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Boost regulator",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Current-mode control with an inner loop that limits current on each cycle and an outer loop that sets the output voltage. Once enabled, the NMOS switch closes and the inductor current climbs to a peak of about 0.6 A; the current comparator then holds the switch open for a fixed time so its stored energy moves into the output capacitor. Once the output reaches its set point, nominally 10 V, switching stops until the load pulls the capacitor below it, and then resumes. This start-stop charging leaves a ripple on VO whose size depends on the output capacitor's value and ESR. The regulator is tuned for the parts in the typical application."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Horn turn-on",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "On both parts a high on HRNEN starts the regulator first, and the horn output stays off until VO has reached its nominal 10 V set point, so the drive voltage is present when the horn starts. Measured on the RE46C317 with HORNB carrying 20 mA DC, HORNB switches on about 690 µs after HRNEN goes high."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Series resistance",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Current pulses near 0.8 A in boost mode make the converter sensitive to resistance in the inductor path: at VDD = 2 V and full load the inductor DC resistance and its wiring should total 0.3 Ω or less, the battery 0.5 Ω or less, with at least 10 µF of low-ESR capacitance across the battery."
  },
  {
   "topic": "FEED when unused",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "FEED must be tied to VSS if the horn's feedback electrode is not used."
  }
 ],
